<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="diffeq-notes-3-1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Existence &amp; Uniqueness for Linear ODEs</title>

    <objectives>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Students will be able to...
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <ol>
            <li><p> Recall and apply theorems related to existence and uniquness of solutions to linear ODEs. </p></li>
            <li><p> Distinguish between particular, complementary, fundamental, and general solutions. </p></li>
        </ol>
    </objectives>

    <definition xml:id="def-linear-operator">
        <statement>
            <p>
                We say <m>L</m> is a <alert>linear operator</alert> if, for all constants <m>\alpha, \beta</m> and all functions <m>f,g</m> we have the property that <md> L[\alpha f(x)  + \beta g(x)] = \alpha L[f(x)] + \beta L[g(x)]. </md> 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The derivative is a linear operator, since we have <md> \cal{D}[\alpha f(x) + \beta g(x) ] = \deriv{}{x}[\alpha f(x) + \beta g(x) ] = \alpha f'(x) + \beta g'(x) = \alpha \mathcal{D}[f(x)] + \beta \mathcal{D}[g(x)]. </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-superposition">
        <title>Principle of Superposition</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>y_1, \ldots, y_k</m> are all solutions to an nth-order homogeneous ODE on an interval <m>I</m>. Then, the linear combination <md> y := c_1y_1(x) + c_2y_2(x) + \cdots + c_ky_k(x),</md> is also a solution. 
            </p>
        </statement>

        <proof>
            <p>
                We will prove the case that <m>k=2</m>. Let <m>y_1, y_2</m> solve the equation <md> Ly = 0 </md> and suppose that <m>L</m> is linear. Then <md> L[c_1 y_1 + c_2 y_2] = c_1 L[y_1] + c_2 L[y_2] = 0.</md> Therefore, <m>y</m> is a solution. 
            </p>
        </proof>
    </theorem>
    

    <theorem xml:id="thm-ivp-existence">
        <title>Existence and Uniqueness of Solutions to IVPs</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider the <m>n^{\text{th}}</m>-order linear IVP 
                <md>
                    <mrow> a_n(x) \deriv{^ny}{x^n} + a_{n-1}(x) \deriv{^{(n-1)}y}{x^{(n-1)}} + \cdots + a_1(x) \deriv{y}{x} + a_0(x) y = \amp f(x) </mrow>
                    <mrow> y(x_0) = y_0, y'(x_0) = y_1, \ldots, y^{(n-1)}(x_0) = \amp  y_{n-1}. </mrow>
                </md>
                If <m>a_n, \ldots, a_1, a_0</m>, and <m>f</m> be continuous on an interval <m>I</m> containing <m>x_0</m> with the property that <m>a_n(x) \neq 0</m> for all <m>x \in I </m>. Then, there is a unique solution <m>y</m> to the IVP.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <p>
        Why talk about it?
    </p>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider the second-order linear ODE <md> x'' + 16x = 0. </md> The general solution to this equation has the form <md> x(t) = c_1 \cos(4t) + c_2 \sin(4t). </md> A <alert>boundary condition</alert> is similar to an initial condition, but may be given at any input. Consider the following cases. 
                <ol>
                    <li><p> Under BCs <m> x(0) = x\left(\frac{\pi}{8}\right) = 0 </m>, we get a single unique solution as expected (existence and uniquness).</p></li>
                    <li><p> Under BCs <m> x(0) = x\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right) = 0 </m>, we get infinitely-many solutions (existence only). </p></li>
                    <li><p> Under BCs <m> x(0) = 0, x\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right) = 1 </m>, we get no solutions. </p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>

    <p> 
        Recall an nth-order linear ODE is <alert>homogeneous</alert> if it has not forcing function. 
    </p> 

    <definition xml:id="def-linearly-independent">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The functions <m>f_1, f_2, \ldots, f_n</m> are said to be <alert> linearly independent </alert> on the interval <m>I</m> provided <md> c_1 f_1 + c_2 f_2 + \cdots + c_n f_n = 0 </md> for all <m>x \in I</m> only when <m> c_1 = c_2 = \cdots = c_n = 0 </m>. Otherwise, we say the functions are <alert> linearly dependent </alert>. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The functions <md> f_1(x) = 1, f_2(x) = x, f_3(x) = 4-3x </md> are linearly dependent since <md> 4 f_1(x) - 3 f_2(x) - f_3(x) = 0 </md> for all <m>x</m>. The functiond <md> g_1(x) = 1, g_2(x) = x, g_3(x) = x^2 </md> are linearly independent, since the only way to guarantee <md> c_1 + c_2x + c_3x^2 = 0 </md> for all <m>x</m> is to take <m>c_1 = c_2 = c_3 = 0 </m>.  
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>
    
    <definition xml:id="def-Wronskian">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m> f_1, \ldots, f_n </m> be at least <m>n-1</m> times differentiable. Their <alert> Wronskian </alert> is <md> W(f_1, \ldots, f_n) = \begin{vmatrix} f_1 \amp f_2 \amp \cdots \amp f_n \\ f_1' \amp f_2' \amp \cdots \amp f_n' \\ \vdots \amp \vdots \amp \ddots \amp \vdots \\ f^{(n-1)}_1 \amp f^{(n-1)}_2 \amp \cdots \amp f^{(n-1)}_n \end{vmatrix} </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-lin-indep-criteria">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The functions <m> f_1, \ldots, f_n </m> are linearly-independent on <m> I </m> if and only if their Wronskian is nonzero on <m> I </m>. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <definition xml:id="def-fundamental-sols">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>y_1, \ldots, y_n </m> solve an nth-order homogeneous linear differential equation on <m>I</m>. We call them <alert> fundamental solutions </alert> if they are lienarly independent. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-existence-linear-homog">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Every linear nth-order homogeneous ODE has a fundamental set of solutions. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-general-sol-linear-homog">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>y_1, \ldots, y_n</m> be fundamental solutions to an nth-order linear homogeneous ODE on <m> I </m>. The <alert> general solution </alert> of the equation on <m>I</m> is <md> y(x) = c_1 y_1 + \cdots + c_2 y_2 </md>. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <definition xml:id="def-complementary-soln">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <md> \cal{L}[y] = f(x) </md> is an nth-order linear nonhomogeneous ODE. The equation <md> \cal{L}[y] = 0 </md> is called the <alert> complementary equation </alert> to the nonhomogeneous ODE, and its solution is the <alert> complementary solution </alert>. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-general-sol-linear-nonhomog">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>y_p</m> be a particular solution to an nth-order linear nonhomogeneous ODE on <m> I</m> and let <m>y_c</m> be its corresponding complementary solution. Then <md> y(x) = y_c + y_p. </md>  is the <alert> general solution </alert> to the nonhomogeneous equation. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

</section>
